# thirdpart/musl-libc-algorithms.manifest — vendored musl algorithms
#
# Provenance (libc-completion plan T11, "固定 musl 1.2.5 源档来源和 SHA256"):
#   Archive : https://musl.libc.org/releases/musl-1.2.5.tar.gz
#   SHA256  : a9a118bbe84d8764da0ea0d28b3ab3fae8477fc7e4085d90102b8596fc7c75e4
#   License : standard MIT (see musl-libc-algorithms/COPYRIGHT, copied
#             verbatim from the archive's COPYRIGHT file).
#
# What is vendored, where, and how it was adapted
# -----------------------------------------------
# libc/stdlib/floatscan.c
#   from src/internal/floatscan.c (507 lines)
#   adaptation diff (this is the full list; everything else is verbatim):
#     - #include <stdio.h>/<math.h> replaced by the private
#       <stdlib/floatscan.h> + libc's own <errno.h>/<ctype.h>/<float.h>;
#       `hidden` dropped (the scanner is static to this archive's TU).
#     - FILE* replaced by the memory-only shfile_t pseudo-FILE
#       (libc/include/stdlib/floatscan.h): musl's shgetc/shunget/shcnt/
#       shlim/sh_fromstring macros with __uflow and the real FILE
#       implementation NOT imported — strtod-family input is always a
#       NUL-terminated memory string.  Behaviour difference, deliberate:
#       shgetc answers EOF sticky at the terminating NUL without
#       advancing (musl reads the NUL byte and advances), which keeps
#       musl's shunget bookkeeping balanced and the POSIX endptr exact.
#     - the four math calls are vendored in the same file as static
#       helpers (see below), because OS01's freestanding libc has no
#       libm to answer scalbn/scalbnl/copysignl/fmodl.
#     - errno=EINVAL on no-conversion is SUPPRESSED by the wrappers in
#       libc/stdlib/strtod.c (they save/restore errno around a
#       zero-consumption scan): musl sets EINVAL there, glibc/POSIX
#       practice and our T10 strtox discipline preserve errno.  The
#       scanner itself is untouched.
#     - no-conversion detection moved to the WRAPPERS: the scanner's
#       EINVAL flags it, the wrappers answer endptr = nptr and restore
#       errno (musl leaves endptr past the whitespace and keeps
#       EINVAL; glibc answers nptr + untouched errno, and the oracle
#       table pins that).
#     - subnormal ERANGE raised to glibc's practice: EVERY inexact
#       subnormal result answers ERANGE (musl only some).  EXACT
#       subnormals stay silent — "0x1p-1074" answers errno=0 like
#       glibc — so the check applies to decimal inputs only.
#     - "nan(<n-chars>)" payload support added (mk_nan): the payload
#       digits are read as a DECIMAL number into the low mantissa
#       bits with the sign bit honoured ("nan(123)" / "-nan" match
#       glibc bit-for-bit).  musl returns a payload-less positive NaN
#       here; __builtin_nanf(payload) was rejected because a runtime
#       string turns the builtin into a libm nanf() call and OS01 has
#       no libm.  Non-digit n-chars end the payload (simplification;
#       the test table only exercises digit payloads).
#     - the strtod/strtof wrappers narrow NaNs BIT-EXACTLY (sign +
#       payload rebuilt from the 80-bit mantissa): the x87 narrowing
#       instruction would otherwise drop the payload of "nan(123)"
#       when producing a float/double.
#
#   static helpers inside libc/stdlib/floatscan.c (same MIT license):
#     - copysignl  from src/math/copysignl.c   (LDBL_MANT_DIG==64 branch)
#     - scalbn     from src/math/scalbn.c
#     - scalbnl    from src/math/scalbnl.c     (LDBL_MANT_DIG==64 branch)
#     - fmodl      from src/math/fmodl.c       (LDBL_MANT_DIG==64 branch)
#     with libm.h's union ldshape (little-endian 80-bit x87 layout)
#     trimmed in, isnan(x) written as x != x, fabsl/copysign delegated
#     to compiler builtins (no libcall on x86_64).
#
# Patches to the module after vendoring
# -------------------------------------
# None yet.  If musl fixes a floatscan bug after 1.2.5, the patch is
# reviewed against the adaptation diff above (only the listed deltas
# are OS01-specific; everything else applies cleanly) and this manifest
# gains a dated entry describing what was taken and why.
#
# Not applicable / deliberately not imported
# ------------------------------------------
# src/internal/stdio_impl.h, __uflow, the real FILE machinery — replaced
# by the memory pseudo-FILE (see above).
# src/internal/shgetc.c/shgetc.h — folded into the pseudo-FILE macros in
# libc/include/stdlib/floatscan.h (the __shlim/__shgetc functions only
# serve real-FILE and width-limited scanf, neither of which this port
# has).
# wcsto*-family callers (need real FILE pushback semantics) — OS01 has
# no wchar layer.
# musl's locale-dependent scanning — musl floatscan has none.
